\title{Strong Nullstellensatz}
\taxon{Theorem}
\date{2023-10-01}
\author{JB00}
\import{macros}

\p{Let #{f, f_1, \ldots, f_m \in \mathbb{F}[X_1, \ldots, X_n]} be such that #{f} vanishes at all the common zeros of #{f_1, \ldots, f_m \in \mathbb{F}[X]}. Then some power of #{f} is in the ideal generated by #{f_1, \ldots, f_m}.}

\proof{
\p{\em{Proof (the Rabinowitsch Trick).} To see that the ‘weak form’ implies this ‘strong form’, let #{X_0} be an additional variable, and consider the #{m + 1} polynomials #{f_1, \ldots, f_m} and #{X_0f - 1}. These #{m + 1} polynomials in our #{n + 1} variables #{X_0, X_1, \ldots, X_n} have no common zero, since if #{f_i(x) = f_i(x_0, \ldots, x_n) = 0} for #{i = 1, \ldots, m} and some #{x \in \mathbb{F}^{n+1}}, then #{f(x) = 0}, so #{x_0f(x) - 1 = -1 \neq 0}. The ‘weak form’ tells us that Bézout’s equation (1) is solvable, i.e. there are polynomials #{g_0, \ldots, g_m} in #{\widetilde{X} = (X_0, \ldots, X_n)} such that}

##{1 = \sum_{i=1}^m g_i(\widetilde{X})f_i(X) + g_0(\widetilde{X}) \cdot (X_0f(X) - 1).}

\p{Setting #{X_0 = 1/f(X)}, this identity becomes}

##{1 = \sum_{i=1}^m g_i\bigg( \frac{1}{f(X)}, X_1, \ldots, X_n \bigg)f_i = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^m h_if_i(X)}{f(X)^k},}

\p{where #{k} is the maximal power of #{X_0} that occurs in the monomials of the #{g_i}, so that #{h_i \in \mathbb{F}[X]}. Hence #{f^k = \sum_{i=1}^m h_if_i}, as required.}
}
